language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ Find n(n-1)/6 triples of distinct elements from 1..n such that any two triples have at most one common element (ternary Steiner system of order n).
given n : int(1..)
$ sets[r,j] is true if element j is part of triple r
find sets : matrix indexed by [int(1..n*(n-1)/6), int(1..n)] of bool

such that
$ each triple consists of exactly three distinct elements
forAll r : int(1..n*(n-1)/6) . (sum j : int(1..n) . toInt(sets[r,j])) = 3,
$ any two triples have at most one common element
forAll r : int(1..n*(n-1)/6) . forAll s : int(r+1..n*(n-1)/6) . (sum j : int(1..n) . toInt(sets[r,j] /\ sets[s,j])) <= 1,
$ symmetry breaking: triples are a set, so the rows are kept in lexicographic order
forAll r : int(1..n*(n-1)/6-1) . sets[r,..] <=lex sets[r+1,..],
$ redundant: every element lies in (n-1)/2 triples, since every pair of elements lies in exactly one triple
forAll j : int(1..n) . (sum r : int(1..n*(n-1)/6) . toInt(sets[r,j])) = (n-1)/2
